Approach 3: Single Stack ⭐ (Most Optimal)
Intuition

Instead of maintaining another stack,

store the previous minimum inside the same stack.

Whenever a new minimum arrives:

Push the old minimum.
Update minimum.
Push current value.

When popping:

If popped value equals current minimum,

pop again to restore the previous minimum.

Java (Your Code)
class MinStack {

    int min = Integer.MAX_VALUE;

    Stack<Integer> st = new Stack<>();

    public MinStack() {

    }

    public void push(int val) {

        if (val <= min) {

            st.push(min);

            min = val;
        }

        st.push(val);
    }

    public void pop() {

        if (st.pop() == min) {

            min = st.pop();
        }
    }

    public int top() {

        return st.peek();
    }

    public int getMin() {

        return min;
    }
}

